            <ArticleTitle>Transient increase in wall thickness of the left ventricular apex after stunned myocardium: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>122-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A case of transiently increased wall thickness in the left ventricular apex after stunned myocardium due to severe attack of vasospastic angina is described. &quot;Ace of spades&quot; configuration, documented by left ventriculogram and increased apical wall thickness of the left ventricle, as revealed by two-dimensional echocardiograms returned to normal in 2 months. This transiently increased left ventricular mass may have been due to myocardial edema, which is occasionally seen in the recovery course of active myocarditis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
